    "src_change": {
      "file": "src/qkv2x/features/dataset.py",
      "additions": [
        "window.mode = 'count'(default) | 'time'",
        "window.span_s / window.stride_s (seconds, time mode)",
        "build_dataset(..., fixed_spans=<rx_node, claimed_id, t_start, t_end>) -- evaluates EXACTLY those spans, no min_messages filter, no onset filter, order preserved, meta['n_msgs'] emitted so thin/empty spans are visible"
      ],
      "default_path": "byte-identical (asserted in step A on all 90,553 dev_grid windows against runs/dev_grid/features.parquet)",
      "unit_tests": "tests/test_fixed_spans.py"
    },
    "span_definition": "global half-open grid [k*1.0, k*1.0+1.0) seconds per (RSU receiver, claimed identity) stream; span_s == stride_s so the timeline is tiled exactly and each message belongs to one span",
    "span_duration_rationale": "the published count windows are 10 messages at a 10 Hz nominal BSM rate; measured on runs/dev_grid their duration is median 0.9931 s (q10 0.8862, q90 3.5840) with observed within-stream inter-arrival median 0.1104 s, so a 1.0 s span carries the same nominal 10 messages -- 1.0 s is the count window's own duration, not an assumption. The q90 tail (long windows) exists because a count window stretches across reception gaps; a time span cannot, which is precisely why it is loss-invariant.",
    "population_freezing": "spans, labels, attack names and groups are derived ONCE from the p=0 build and reused unchanged at every loss level; only the FEATURES are recomputed. Labels recomputed under loss are reported (label_flips) but never applied, so n_spans and n_pos are constant by construction.",
    "loss_injection": "build_dataset's own rule, unmodified: after the receiver_set='rsu' filter, rx = rx[default_rng(drop_seed).random(len(rx)) >= p] with drop_seed = 7 + run_idx (the published packetloss.py seeding). Identical seed and row count at every level => the retained message sets are NESTED in p (verified: True).",
    "feature_recomputation": "message-level features are recomputed from the surviving messages (so RSSI/kinematic/Sybil statistics degrade too), and window rate features use the span's KNOWN duration D: rate_ratio = n_msgs/(D*rate), pdr = n_msgs/(D*rate + 1). Empty spans get an all-zero feature vector and are retained as detection failures rather than deleted.",
    "split": "one GroupShuffleSplit on the FROZEN population (test_frac=0.3, val_frac=0.15, seed=7, group_by=sender), reused at every loss level, so train/val/test spans are the SAME objects at every p",
    "matched_n": "all arms train on the quantum max_train=3000 group-wise cap (matched-N, exactly as the published packetloss curve's non-__full rows). A classical@full ceiling arm was NOT run (cost); the published curve's __full rows remain the scaling context.",
    "arms": {
      "retrained": "detector refit at each level on that level's train spans (reducer refit too) -- 'operator retrains for the channel'",
      "frozen_p0": "the p=0 detector and p=0 reducer deployed unchanged, only the input features degrade -- 'deployed detector'. CLASSICAL ONLY: run_model does not return a fitted quantum model, so a frozen quantum arm is NOT computed here (IRRECOVERABLE from the current API without a further src change, which this item does not authorise beyond dataset.py)."
    },
    "operating_point": "threshold_protocol's protocol: thr = quantile(p=0 VALIDATION benign scores, 0.99) (numpy linear interpolation), positive iff score > thr. Derived once per (arm, model, level) on the p=0 validation spans and APPLIED UNCHANGED to the level-p test spans, so the realised test FPR is free to drift. Validation spans are group-capped to 8000 rows (seed 4242) for memory. A retuned-on-test threshold is reported as recall_at_1pct_fpr_retuned (upper bound, never headline).",
    "ci_method": "grouped percentile bootstrap, B=500, seed=20260807: test transmitters resampled with replacement; the operating threshold is held fixed (it is not a function of the test rows); CI = [2.5, 97.5] percentiles",
